&lt;p&gt;Points: 8&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 1&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50364529</guid><pubDate>Wed, 04 Nov 2026 12:14:23 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Show HN: Rivermark, a superfast AI graphic design canvas</title><link>https://rivermark.ai/</link><description>&lt;p&gt;Hi HN,&lt;p&gt;Agents can nowadays design UI very competently, but the wider question of graphic design is largely unsolved.&lt;p&gt;On one side, you have image models like ChatGPT images, which are high-quality but recognizable and barely editable. On the other hand, you can use LLMs and accept results that, well, look like UI.&lt;p&gt;I've been working on Rivermark for a few months now to tackle this issue. It's an infinite canvas with an agent that excels at creating on-brand visual content for socials, marketing, and to some extent print. The agent is extremely context-aware and fast (we call it a "short horizon" agent). It will ingest anything and run with it - from Google Sheets copy/paste to .zip files with brandkits or photo sets. Everything is editable manually like in Figma too.&lt;p&gt;We found it works best for people who are not designers but who know what looks good. MCP features to connect it to your agents are coming soon.&lt;p&gt;We're powered by superfast inference at almost 1000 tokens per second and a custom realtime harness, meaning the agent is non-blocking and always responsive, and even has a non-turn-based voice mode that you can just talk into.&lt;p&gt;We have some teams and early power users shipping work with it and are learning a lot. There have been numerous engineering and product challenges to even get here - I'd be happy to talk about them!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
